ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 731.8899999999999864 Td
(thesis depends on agents being real and operational, not aspirational.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 707.8899999999999864 Td
(The planning module. When an agent receives a goal \(e.g., "send 5 KAS to this address and confirm) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 693.8899999999999864 Td
(receipt"\), it does not execute blindly. It first generates a plan: a JSON array of discrete steps, each with a) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 679.8899999999999864 Td
(type \(navigate, click, type, read, wait, verify\), a target, and a success condition. The plan is generated by) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 665.8899999999999864 Td
(an LLM call with a rigid JSON schema enforcing that the output is a valid plan. The schema is not a) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 651.8899999999999864 Td
(suggestion; it is a contract. If the LLM returns malformed JSON, the planner retries. This is the same) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 637.8899999999999864 Td
(pattern used in production agent frameworks — plan-then-execute, with schema validation as the) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 623.8899999999999864 Td
(guardrail.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 599.8899999999999864 Td
(The observe-think-act loop. Once a plan is generated, the agent executes it step by step. For each step,) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 585.8899999999999864 Td
(the loop is: \(1\) Observe — read the current state of the application iframe \(via the injected bridge script,) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 571.8899999999999864 Td
(which exposes the DOM structure, element positions, and text content to the agent\); \(2\) Think — decide) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 557.8899999999999864 Td
(whether the current step's preconditions are met and what the next action is \(an LLM call with the) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 543.8899999999999864 Td
(observation and the current plan step as context\); \(3\) Act — execute the action \(navigate to a URL, click) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 529.8899999999999864 Td
(an element by selector, type text into a field, scroll, or wait\). After each action, the loop returns to) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 515.8899999999999864 Td
(Observe. This is the canonical agent control loop, implemented and operational.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 491.8899999999999864 Td
(The verification layer. After the plan's steps are executed, the agent verifies completion. Verification is) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 477.8899999999999864 Td
(not "the last step ran without error." Verification is "the expected post-condition is observable." For a) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 463.8899999999999864 Td
("send KAS" goal, the post-condition is "the transaction appears in the address's transaction history with) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 449.8899999999999864 Td
(the expected amount." The agent queries the transaction history \(via the getKaspaTransactionHistory) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 435.8899999999999864 Td
(backend function\), looks for the transaction, and reports success or failure. This is what distinguishes an) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 421.8899999999999864 Td
(agent from a script: the agent confirms its own work against ground truth.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 397.8899999999999864 Td
(The bridge script. The agent does not operate applications via a private API. It operates them via the) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 383.8899999999999864 Td
(same DOM a human user would interact with — by injecting a script \(agentBridgeListener.js\) into) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 369.8899999999999864 Td
(application iframes that listens for postMessage commands from the agent runtime and executes them) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 355.8899999999999864 Td
(against the live DOM. This means the agent can operate any application that renders in a browser, not) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 341.8899999999999864 Td
(just applications with agent-specific APIs. This is a deliberate design choice: it means the application) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 327.8899999999999864 Td
(library does not need to be "agent-enabled" — every web application is, by default, agent-operable. The) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 313.8899999999999864 Td
(bridge script is the universal adapter.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 289.8899999999999864 Td
(The reasoning log. As the agent executes, it surfaces its internal state: each step's status \(pending,) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 275.8899999999999864 Td
(running, completed, failed\), the thought it generated at each decision point, the action it took, and the) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 261.8899999999999864 Td
(result. This log is rendered in the UI as a live, animated feed. The reasoning log exists for two reasons:) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 247.8899999999999864 Td
(\(1\) transparency — a human observer can see what the agent is doing and why, which is a prerequisite) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 233.8899999999999864 Td
(for trusting the agent with real value transfers; \(2\) debugging — when an agent fails, the log shows) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 219.8899999999999864 Td
(where and why, enabling iteration on the plan or the application. The reasoning log is the audit trail.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 195.8899999999999864 Td
(The economics of agent execution. Each agent execution has a cost: LLM API calls for planning and) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 181.8899999999999864 Td
(thinking, Kaspa transaction fees for any on-chain actions, and compute time on whatever host runs the) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 167.8899999999999864 Td
(agent loop. In the current implementation, these costs are borne by the application operator. In the) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 153.8899999999999864 Td
(Agent Internet, these costs are borne by the agent's wallet, which must be funded with KAS to pay for) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.137 g
56. 139.8899999999999864 Td
(compute, LLM access, and transaction fees. An agent with an empty wallet cannot act. This is the) Tj
ET
